exit: 0
--- stdout
commandments judge — Scan a codebase and report its sins, grouped by the skill that fixes each. Exit code 1 when sins are found, 3 when a rule could not run.

Usage:
  commandments judge [path]  scan a path — or, with none, the source roots declared in
                             .commandments/config.php
  commandments judge --list  list every detector, grouped by skill

Options:
  --list                         list every detector grouped by the skill that fixes it, and run
                                 none of them
  --skill=NAME                   only run detectors for one skill (group), e.g. spatie-data
  --sin=NAME                     only run detectors for one sin (lenient name match), e.g.
                                 nullable-callback
  --exclude=A,B                  skip findings in paths containing any fragment
  --changes                      only files changed in the working tree (alias: --git)
  --branch[=BASE]                only files new/changed on this branch vs BASE (default: main)
  --repent[=ID]                  only the files listed in a past run's checklist (bare: the latest)
  --parallel=N                   run detectors across N worker processes (default: 8, capped at
                                 cores; 1 = off)
  --ignore-package-requirements  keep package-gated rules even if this project lacks the package
                                 (cross-project calibration)
  --checklist=FILE               write the checklist here (default: your session's sins/sins.md, in
                                 .commandments/sessions/<id>/ or the journal plugin's data folder)
  --no-checklist                 print only, don't write the checklist file
  --benchmark                    time each detector and print the slowest

  With no [path], judge scans the source roots declared by $config->paths(...) in
  .commandments/config.php — auto-detected on first run from your composer.json PSR-4 map (plus
  app/src), so scaffolding like database/, storage/ and config/ is not judged. Run `commandments
  config reindex` to re-detect them, or pass an explicit [path] to scan it directly. Add
  $config->exclude('app/Generated') to subtract a path from ANY run — the tree is still parsed (so
  cross-file rules stay correct) but nothing in it is ever reported or rewritten.

  A rule that BREAKS is skipped so the rest of the run survives — but the run is not green: it
  names the rules that could not run and exits 3 (rather than 0) when nothing else was found,
  because a run missing a rule has not judged what that rule judges.

  Judge writes a Markdown checklist into your session folder (the run prints the exact path). A full
  scan is slow, so judge ONCE and work that file line-by-line, deleting each line as you fix its
  sin; re-run judge at the end to confirm. Files marked @code-commandments-generated are skipped —
  they are regenerated, not hand-authored.

